170
00:21:18,186 --> 00:21:24,451
Then Khyentsey Rinpoche sort of, from kind, loving grandfather to my teacher.

171
00:21:25,672 --> 00:21:32,337
So Khentsey Rinpoche is not only spiritually guiding me, but very, very kind, very kind.

172
00:21:33,472 --> 00:21:34,873
My grandmother was a little bit strict.

173
00:21:36,134 --> 00:21:37,696
So it's a good cop, bad cop.

174
00:21:40,498 --> 00:21:41,539
Maybe not a good example.

175
00:21:43,080 --> 00:21:44,482
Okay, so like that.

176
00:21:44,862 --> 00:22:00,495
So I think of course any teaching or any tradition that even general knowledge we have to rely upon the teacher to learn how to read, how to write.

177
00:22:02,591 --> 00:22:11,674
And then especially when you go into practicing Dharma, then teacher becomes a very important role.

178
00:22:14,075 --> 00:22:27,219
And then from Sutrayana, Mahayana, then Vajrayana, a different teacher plays a different role in your practice.

179
00:22:34,434 --> 00:22:38,157
Oh, you're supposed to translate for me.

180
00:22:38,217 --> 00:22:39,198
I'm speaking English.

181
00:22:42,620 --> 00:22:45,162
OK.

182
00:22:45,202 --> 00:22:46,643
Sort of carried away a little bit.

183
00:22:46,863 --> 00:22:52,147
It's a COVID.

184
00:22:52,227 --> 00:22:57,791
So rely upon a kind of really authentic, special master.

185
00:22:58,211 --> 00:23:03,055
It's very important to get deeper into practice.

186
00:23:03,902 --> 00:23:16,211
because we cannot navigate a sailboat by reading a book or learning how to sail just by watching YouTube or something like that.

187
00:23:17,132 --> 00:23:27,980
So you really need a kind of person-to-person instruction on how to navigate the boat or fly a helicopter just by reading manuals.

188
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:29,341
We cannot do that.

189
00:23:30,342 --> 00:23:31,743
So I think in the spiritual path,

190
00:23:32,607 --> 00:23:38,572
I think we must rely upon an authentic and good teacher.

191
00:23:39,393 --> 00:23:41,674
And then how to find an authentic good teacher?

192
00:23:42,655 --> 00:23:57,948
There is many kinds of instruction taught in the teachings, Sutra, Mahayana, Vajrayana, all teaching emphasize a lot on finding authentic master and teacher.

193
00:23:59,329 --> 00:24:01,771
So one reason why we have mostly

194
00:24:02,458 --> 00:24:29,758
the Tibetan masters here, I think it's because in Tibet, since Buddha Dharma came into Tibet, then I think, I think last time also I said earlier, that the one main kind of purpose of the whole Tibet country was to bring the authentic Dharma into Tibet, where else other countries

195
00:24:30,781 --> 00:24:37,087
focus on development, outer world, development of architecture, all this focus outside.

196
00:24:37,267 --> 00:24:47,035
But in Tibet, our great Tibetan things, around 7th century, all the resources of their country is to bring the authentic Dharma into Tibet.

197
00:24:47,856 --> 00:24:56,083
And there's not any kind of teaching they find in India, but they really went through filtering system to get the real authentic.

198
00:24:56,144 --> 00:24:57,605
And then once they came to Tibet,

199
00:24:58,094 --> 00:25:01,955
And there's many ways of checking whether it is authentic or not.

200
00:25:03,216 --> 00:25:09,218
So they really tried to get a fine, like gold, by beating, by melting it, trying to get it pure.

201
00:25:09,278 --> 00:25:15,580
So that was the whole nation's kind of main purpose was to bring the Tibetan into Tibet.

202
00:25:17,021 --> 00:25:26,724
And then preserving it by, more or less, they say, like 20% of the Tibetan population is focused on preserving that.

203
00:25:27,702 --> 00:25:37,509
20% of the population, Tibetan population, I think we can say monks and nuns, their main focus is to kind of practice and meditate.

204
00:25:40,330 --> 00:25:44,973
So that out of which then comes those great masters.

205
00:25:45,154 --> 00:25:49,156
So it's not something that runs on Tibetan genes.

206
00:25:50,437 --> 00:25:53,079
I don't think it's something to do with Tibetan genes.

207
00:25:53,119 --> 00:25:55,060
It's the circumstances and the situations.

208
00:25:57,417 --> 00:26:00,038
we have one of the best authentic masters.

209
00:26:02,019 --> 00:26:11,743
So, it's like, I sometimes compare like, I shouldn't say Brazil because now we are in England.

210
00:26:12,383 --> 00:26:19,586
So, England, maybe out of all the population in love with football, we have 12 best top players.

211
00:26:20,947 --> 00:26:23,248
Usually I say Brazil because it's a bigger population.

212
00:26:23,685 --> 00:26:34,313
But now I think it's better, say, English football players out of the whole population interested in football, then 12 top players, and compete in the World Cup.

213
00:26:35,474 --> 00:26:43,100
So if we have a meditation World Cup, I'm sure the top Tibetan lamas will win the gold cup.

214
00:26:44,281 --> 00:26:47,283
So that's how they preserve the tradition and then bring it down.

215
00:26:49,665 --> 00:26:51,767
Bring it down, keep it till now, and then

216
00:26:52,355 --> 00:26:54,976
we can rely upon on their teachings.

217
00:26:56,397 --> 00:26:58,098
So Lama Majjheta Tsova Thop.

218
00:26:59,478 --> 00:27:11,724
So especially if we are, I think these four lines, we can kind of correspond to the Sutrayana, Mahayana and Vajrayana level.

219
00:27:13,144 --> 00:27:19,027
So Lama Majjheta Tsova Thop, I think, in order to, of course, every level of the

220
00:27:20,507 --> 00:27:23,169
path we need to rely upon a teacher.

221
00:27:23,829 --> 00:27:32,795
But especially when it comes to the higher tantra or Vajrayana practice, then master plays a very, very important role.

222
00:27:33,616 --> 00:27:42,602
And it is more of how you can receive the authentic teacher.

223
00:27:46,164 --> 00:27:48,766
Especially like Vajrayana empowerment or something,

224
00:27:49,748 --> 00:27:59,593
Then if we are able to see the teacher as an Arhat, then in the Vajrayana teaching it says you receive the blessing from Arhat.

225
00:28:01,014 --> 00:28:06,357
Then if you perceive teacher as a Bodhisattva, then you receive blessing of Bodhisattva.

226
00:28:07,458 --> 00:28:17,163
If you can develop your mind and see the teacher like a Buddha or Guru Rinpoche, then you receive blessing from Guru Rinpoche.

227
00:28:18,236 --> 00:28:27,099
So therefore, when it comes to the Vajrayana practice, then the spiritual master or root teacher becomes very very important role.

228
00:28:28,940 --> 00:28:32,881
So Guru is calling you.

229
00:28:48,523 --> 00:28:55,127
It is said that the compassion, the heartfelt compassion of a spiritual teacher is all-pervasive, like the rays of the sun.

230
00:28:55,147 --> 00:28:57,768
So the rays of the sun, as you know, they are open to all.

231
00:28:57,788 --> 00:29:00,730
However, you have to step into them to receive their warmth.

232
00:29:18,273 --> 00:29:26,576
If you were to remain in the shadows, then even though the light or warmth of the sun is all around you, you'll remain away, absent from it.

233
00:29:49,973 --> 00:30:05,241
So if two things come together, one, as I mentioned then, is the all-pervasive and compassionate warmth of the qualified teacher's blessing.

234
00:30:06,761 --> 00:30:14,065
And if the disciple is open to that, steps out of the shadows into the warmth of that embrace,

235
00:30:14,970 --> 00:30:25,314
Then the two of these things coming together, the compassion of the teacher and the faith or devotion of the disciple, then can cause a spiritual realization to be born.

236
00:30:27,934 --> 00:30:28,935
Today in the Kamsotha,

237
00:30:50,339 --> 00:30:59,206
Relying on the teacher and seeing the teacher as Buddha, it's not Dāna to try to create favor with the teacher.

238
00:30:59,326 --> 00:31:03,149
You're not doing the teacher a favor by seeing them in that way.

239
00:31:03,730 --> 00:31:08,554
Rather, the point of it is to train your own mind, is to subdue your unruly mind.

240
00:31:08,674 --> 00:31:10,415
That's why the teaching is given.

241
00:31:10,455 --> 00:31:12,657
That's why one views the teacher in such a way.

242
00:31:14,475 --> 00:31:44,155
Today, I don't consider myself as a teacher, but I always, people who somehow connect with me and some people who somehow want to receive some of the lineage of Khyentse Rinpoche, then I always tell them that your source of inspiration or Tsa wei  Lama or Root Guru, just consider those who come to me, I always say, think of Dilgo Khyentsey Rinpoche.

243
00:31:44,981 --> 00:31:53,467
as you are kind of a spiritual Guru.

244
00:31:53,768 --> 00:31:59,172
That was English.

245
00:31:59,192 --> 00:32:01,934
This happens a lot since Covid.

246
00:32:04,135 --> 00:32:14,723
So now with the modern technology we can see the video, we can hear his voice, we can see how he looks like,

247
00:32:15,563 --> 00:32:17,304
You can see how he smiles.

248
00:32:18,205 --> 00:32:26,170
Then by reading his biography, thinking about him, then we really feel kind of strong kind of devotion.

249
00:32:27,111 --> 00:32:34,316
Even physically, not with us, I think his spiritual energy is very much, very much always there.

250
00:32:35,577 --> 00:32:44,123
So I always encourage them that me as I'm kind of like intimate in between, you know,

251
00:32:45,492 --> 00:32:46,032
mediator.

252
00:32:47,113 --> 00:32:56,155
So, sort of, since now we need human to transfer, we cannot now use AI to give the artificial intelligence to give the teachings.

253
00:32:57,516 --> 00:33:01,377
So, we still use Zoom sometimes for lung.

254
00:33:02,417 --> 00:33:11,960
So, I'm sort of in between, to transmit like rituals or Wang, when Vajrayana empowerment or Wang, I'm there to sort of

255
00:33:13,074 --> 00:33:18,578
intimate in between, but you should consider like the Longchen Rinpoche as your teacher.

256
00:33:18,938 --> 00:33:23,942
So I tell them, those who come to me, and the same thing with other schools, I think we can do that.

257
00:33:24,983 --> 00:33:36,451
Some people who are inspired by Drubjay Rinpoche, listen to his teachings, watch his videos and all that, and Nubjay Rinpoche, Chabjay Gayden Rinpoche, same thing I think.

258
00:33:38,092 --> 00:33:39,353
They are like really enlightened,

259
00:33:40,311 --> 00:33:55,318
really really enlightened, I'm not sort of making it up, being with them, sitting with them, they're physically no more with us, then we can still kind of feel strong devotion and longing to become like them.

260
00:33:56,238 --> 00:34:06,763
Then they're teaching lineage or some wang or some rituals, then there's some students like myself and of course I'm the maybe

261
00:34:08,170 --> 00:34:12,472
but there are many other students of Khyentsey Rinpoche who are very much alive.

262
00:34:12,712 --> 00:34:19,454
And then you see the lineage from them, and then consider Khyentsey Rinpoche as teaching master, a source for inspiration.

263
00:34:20,695 --> 00:34:22,576
So that's, I think, is very much possible.

264
00:34:23,776 --> 00:34:34,320
And if we can develop like that, and then during Wang, during the empowerment, I always say, tell them that visualize now Vajra master somehow plays an important role in the Wang.

265
00:34:34,380 --> 00:34:35,921
So visualize me as Khyentsey Rinpoche.

266
00:34:36,621 --> 00:34:40,624
and imagine that you're receiving the blessing from Khyentsey Rinpoche, directly from him.

267
00:34:45,307 --> 00:34:54,293
So, Lama Majjhitaatu Sovata, then that is how we get really inspire Khyentsey Rinpoche or your teacher, spiritual teacher.

268
00:34:55,274 --> 00:35:00,798
Time to time, I think it's very good to remind, because the teacher represents the Dharma.

269
00:35:01,539 --> 00:35:04,220
When you think of teacher, you think nothing but his teachings.

270
00:35:05,601 --> 00:35:06,362
Something else like

271
00:35:08,342 --> 00:35:16,265
If you had fun with some friend in a normal life, when you think of that friend, you remember the fun moment.

272
00:35:17,345 --> 00:35:20,206
So here, when you think of a teacher, then you think of his teaching.

273
00:35:21,626 --> 00:35:22,807
Lama Majjhepati Svarupa.

274
00:35:23,047 --> 00:35:24,247
Don't try to remember again.

275
00:35:25,928 --> 00:35:34,751
There is some kind of Tibetan custom that some senior lamas, out of nothing, suddenly they'll say,

276
00:35:37,115 --> 00:35:40,518
I remember Guru Rinpoche, which is very nice, beautiful.

277
00:35:40,538 --> 00:35:44,161
We used to tease one Lama every time he would say, Oh, Jin Jin.

278
00:35:45,362 --> 00:35:46,663
And we used to tease him a lot.

279
00:35:46,703 --> 00:35:47,523
He stopped saying it.

280
00:35:48,364 --> 00:35:52,447
And then we feel very bad that we made him stop that.

281
00:35:53,808 --> 00:35:57,791
So I think it has to be kind of, it has to be like part of your thing.

282
00:35:57,872 --> 00:36:01,715
In certain circumstances, if something arises, Oh, Jin Jin.

283
00:36:07,064 --> 00:36:10,366
I can tell a joke, no?

284
00:36:10,647 --> 00:36:22,274
So there was a Western Buddhist, Western European whose background raised in Christian kind of environment and became Buddhist.

285
00:36:23,095 --> 00:36:36,103
So then he was flying in the plane and then suddenly the plane went through a very turbulent situation and he is praying to Buddha, Guru Rinpoche, Guru Rinpoche, Guru Rinpoche, Buddha, Buddha, Buddha, Buddha.

286
00:36:36,875 --> 00:36:38,315
and then landed safely.

287
00:36:38,836 --> 00:36:51,799
And he said, thank God.

288
00:36:53,580 --> 00:36:56,861
Just to wake you up.

289
00:36:57,961 --> 00:37:02,742
So now, I think the outer Lama, I'm talking a lot about outer Lama, outer Lama.

290
00:37:03,042 --> 00:37:04,483
The reason, main reason why

291
00:37:06,348 --> 00:37:08,169
We have to rely upon the outer Lama.

292
00:37:08,249 --> 00:37:16,932
The ultimate Lama is none other than your own Buddha nature within you.

293
00:37:17,732 --> 00:37:26,615
Without reliance on the outer conventional Lama, then the ultimate Lama, the nature of one's mind, cannot be revealed or realized.

294
00:37:36,717 --> 00:37:44,441
Until you recognize the ultimate nature of your mind, the inner Lama, then it's imperative to rely upon the outer conventional Lama.

295
00:37:56,585 --> 00:38:04,028
offering salutation and prostration to him, singing his praises, making offerings and so forth, receiving teachings and putting them into practice.

296
00:38:04,348 --> 00:38:08,490
The sole purpose of this is to then realize the inner Buddha nature.

297
00:38:18,702 --> 00:38:27,987
Having realized the ultimate inner nature, the inner Lama, if you will, then there is no need to continually rely upon the outer Lama any longer.

298
00:38:28,187 --> 00:38:28,427
Why?

299
00:38:28,767 --> 00:38:30,168
Because the job is done.

300
00:38:30,468 --> 00:38:35,571
He's introduced you to the inner Lama and you've recognized your own Buddha nature, the inner Lama.

301
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:57,529
So the second line of our four lines, do not let your mind be distracted, look into its very essence.

302
00:38:58,310 --> 00:39:02,913
So this points then to the ultimate nature of spiritual or meditation practice.

303
00:39:03,593 --> 00:39:10,879
The ultimate nature, the Buddha nature is introduced and then having received that introduction or that instruction then one

304
00:39:11,618 --> 00:39:16,603
applies it to oneself, one digests it if you like, such that it becomes a part of who we are.

305
00:39:32,851 --> 00:39:38,613
And so, when we think of ourselves, we're a composite of body, speech, and mind.

306
00:39:39,513 --> 00:39:41,074
And so that much, in a way, is obvious.

307
00:39:41,414 --> 00:39:46,936
And of the three, mind is that which is important, particularly for spiritual practice.

308
00:39:47,336 --> 00:39:49,297
As Kevji Dujunmohse used to say,

309
00:39:50,215 --> 00:39:58,061
From one perspective, the Dhamma is quite difficult to practice because one needs to gather many things together and one needs to learn a lot and do a lot, so to speak.

310
00:39:58,481 --> 00:40:02,704
From another angle, it's really quite easy because it's all to do with the mind.

311
00:40:20,422 --> 00:40:23,983
So as we read here, do not let your mind be distracted.

312
00:40:24,003 --> 00:40:28,685
So when we are attempting to practice meditation, the difficulty we face is distraction, isn't it?

313
00:40:43,979 --> 00:41:01,945
So when we talk about continuing, or strengthening, deepening, if you will, meditation practice, it's imperative not to be distracted by the objects that are proper to us through the six senses, the five sense consciousness, consciousnesses and the mental consciousness.

314
00:41:02,506 --> 00:41:12,129
But rather to, as opposed to looking outwardly, rather one should look within, look at the essence of experience or essence of the of the mind and remain with that.

315
00:41:14,644 --> 00:41:18,147
In the past, we used to go to the temple to pray, but now we don't go to the temple to pray.

316
00:41:18,787 --> 00:41:22,610
We go to the temple to pray, but we don't go to the temple to pray.

317
00:41:22,630 --> 00:41:27,533
We go to the temple to pray, but we don't go to the temple to pray.

318
00:41:27,573 --> 00:41:31,596
We go to the temple to pray, but we don't go to the temple to pray.

319
00:41:37,571 --> 00:41:51,577
And so to continually investigate the nature of one's mind, to look, as we read, into its very essence, this will then deliver us the result of spiritual practice, the understanding of the ultimate, if you will.

320
00:41:52,217 --> 00:42:06,622
However, if we neglect this practice and we continually, for example, recite prayers, mantras, such as the six-syllable Om Mani Padme Hum mantra, etc in a way, we're given to distraction and not much benefit is going to come of that.

321
00:42:06,702 --> 00:42:10,243
For the real practice is to be devoid of distraction.

322
00:42:20,952 --> 00:42:35,515
And so, if we think of ourselves as a kind of Buddhist practitioner, and we go to the temple, and perhaps we sit in the meditation room, we recite kind of prayers and mantras and so forth,

323
00:42:51,892 --> 00:42:54,595
Do we actually stop and sit in meditation?

324
00:42:55,916 --> 00:42:57,477
This is a big question, isn't it?

325
00:42:58,378 --> 00:43:07,447
Here it's imperative to actually stop the distraction of doing something externally and look within and abide within the nature.

326
00:43:07,747 --> 00:43:14,574
At that time, when we meditate, we can talk of meditation session and then a post meditation session.

327
00:43:15,627 --> 00:43:34,180
during the post-meditation session, which for the majority of us is considerably longer than the actual meditation session, it's important to continually check your mind, to look within and to look at the nature of thoughts and emotions, the experiences of life, if you will.

328
00:43:34,761 --> 00:43:45,068
And the more familiar one becomes with this, the shorter the gap between the meditation session and the post-meditation session, until the two merge.

329
00:43:47,458 --> 00:43:55,743
For example, if you go to Rangsema, Rangyong, or Rangotu, you will find that there is a place called Tongbo.

330
00:43:56,664 --> 00:43:59,205
In Tajikistan, there is a place called Semchi.

331
00:43:59,245 --> 00:44:02,367
In Semchi, there is a place called Ngojun.

332
00:44:02,407 --> 00:44:04,648
In Mayang, there is a place called Jundu.

333
00:44:05,409 --> 00:44:08,791
In Mayang, there is a place called Jongyong.

334
00:44:08,911 --> 00:44:13,054
In Semchi, there is a place called Yangne, and in Yangtuk, there is a place called Kanangtuk.

335
00:44:13,074 --> 00:44:15,195
And in Rangbute, there is a place called Lennar.

336
00:44:17,005 --> 00:44:18,845
Do not let your mind be distracted.

337
00:44:19,206 --> 00:44:20,786
Look into its very nature.

338
00:44:21,986 --> 00:44:24,507
This is a very profound instruction.

339
00:44:24,527 --> 00:44:26,267
These are very high words.

340
00:44:27,628 --> 00:44:42,711
Really, to understand their meaning, one should receive guidance on meditation or semchit from one's teacher, leading to an introduction to the nature, or awakened, if you will, nature of mind.

341
00:44:43,051 --> 00:44:45,992
Having received those introductions, it's imperative to

342
00:44:47,211 --> 00:45:02,015
digest them well such that one isn't distracted but continues to maintain one's nature that which has been introduced and the more that one becomes familiar with this practice the less one will be given to distraction.

343
00:45:14,016 --> 00:45:22,043
So the first two lines, do not forget the guru, pray to him at all times, do not let your mind be distracted, look into its very essence.

344
00:45:22,743 --> 00:45:26,646
We can say that these constitute the path of mantra, or secret mantra, tantra, if you will.

345
00:45:44,310 --> 00:45:49,494
Now the third line, do not forget death, let it spur you on to the Dharma.

346
00:45:50,194 --> 00:46:00,982
Here, the mention is made of the nature of a conventional life, if you will, and is brought up so as to induce a sense of renunciation.

347
00:46:01,302 --> 00:46:08,967
As such, we could say that this third line belongs to what we might call the Theravada or Hinayana, if you will, tradition.

348
00:46:14,574 --> 00:46:16,995
The word that we translate as renunciation

349
00:46:34,968 --> 00:46:42,811
really means a desire to definitely emerge, to definitely emerge from samsara or contaminated existence.

350
00:46:43,291 --> 00:46:49,253
And for that to happen, one needs to reflect upon the nature of contaminated existence or samsara.

351
00:46:49,453 --> 00:46:56,655
If one does so, then naturally a wish to leave or to emerge from that situation will arise within us.

352
00:46:57,136 --> 00:47:04,178
Without such an understanding of the nature of things, conventional nature of things, then a desire to leave it will not arise that easily.

353
00:47:05,239 --> 00:47:14,966
So if we look around us, we look at the environment within which we find ourselves, and we look at the sentient beings living

354
00:47:30,153 --> 00:47:34,415
within said environment, none of them are going to remain forever.

355
00:47:34,835 --> 00:47:39,517
Everything, down to its molecular makeup, is in the nature of change.

356
00:47:41,318 --> 00:47:43,859
That's when we think that everything is in the nature of change.

357
00:47:43,879 --> 00:47:45,079
Impermanence means not permanent, doesn't it?

358
00:47:45,119 --> 00:47:45,840
In the nature of change.

359
00:47:45,860 --> 00:47:47,200
When we think of experience,

360
00:47:59,779 --> 00:48:02,120
We are going to experience that which is impermanent.

361
00:48:02,280 --> 00:48:07,763
As such, it's not going to allow us any permanent or enduring satisfaction.

362
00:48:08,144 --> 00:48:11,405
And this is the meaning of dukkha, which is often translated as suffering.

363
00:48:12,666 --> 00:48:13,767
When we think about life,

364
00:48:30,984 --> 00:48:40,659
Here, it is imperative to think of what is really useful, because if we look at our life, is there any lasting or enduring benefit to anything we do?

365
00:48:41,220 --> 00:48:46,528
If we reflect on this point again and again, we'll see that it's not really worth our time.

366
00:48:47,235 --> 00:48:54,617
And thereby, we come to see that life in and of itself cannot give us any lasting or enduring satisfaction.

367
00:48:54,817 --> 00:48:58,438
As such, it's empty of enduring essence, if you will.

368
00:48:58,778 --> 00:49:02,479
With that understanding, then it spurs us on to Dharma practice.

369
00:49:16,470 --> 00:49:17,130
I'm not sure.

370
00:49:48,718 --> 00:49:50,519
here, do not forget death.

371
00:49:51,660 --> 00:49:51,920
Why?

372
00:49:53,001 --> 00:49:58,064
Let this contemplation spur you on to the Dharma.

373
00:49:58,944 --> 00:50:10,291
So the point of remembering impermanence and death is not a morbid fantasy, but rather the point of it is to avoid procrastination.

374
00:50:11,091 --> 00:50:15,174
Because when we think of our own Dharma practice, more often than not, we've given in

375
00:50:15,943 --> 00:50:17,624
We give in to procrastination.

376
00:50:17,864 --> 00:50:20,306
I can't do something today, so perhaps I'll put it off.

377
00:50:20,346 --> 00:50:25,029
I'll do it tomorrow, or the next day, or next week, when I have the time, funds, etc.

378
00:50:25,870 --> 00:50:32,455
Here we're continually putting off, thinking that we have ample time to do something later, when that's not necessarily the case.

379
00:50:33,535 --> 00:50:39,840
So, for example, when we think of riding a horse, if we would like the horse to go quickly, we use the whip.

380
00:50:40,600 --> 00:50:44,583
And the whip then makes the horse go faster, gallop faster.

381
00:50:45,154 --> 00:50:57,958
So similarly, we should use the reflection on impermanence and death to spur on or to enhance or speed up, if you like, our efforts at putting the Dharma into practice.

382
00:51:12,156 --> 00:51:15,918
And the last line, do not forget sentient beings.

383
00:51:15,938 --> 00:51:21,220
With compassion, dedicate your merit to them and make prayers of aspiration.

384
00:51:39,714 --> 00:51:58,385
So we're reminded in the last line, which kind of epitomizes the greater vehicle or the Mahayana teachings, we're reminded not to forget sentient beings, but rather to reflect upon them and their plight, their nature, if you will.

385
00:51:59,305 --> 00:52:07,830
Without remembering the kindness of sentient beings, there's very little chance we'll give rise to the wish to awaken for their benefit or bodhicitta.

386
00:52:08,624 --> 00:52:13,007
Accordingly, one should recall sentient beings continually, recall their kindness to us.

387
00:52:13,547 --> 00:52:24,895
Indeed, we can utilize the various meditation techniques of reflecting on how each and every sentient being has, at some point or another, had the experience of being a kind parent to us.

388
00:52:25,435 --> 00:52:30,818
To recall that kindness, wish to repay it, meditating on love, compassion, and so forth.

389
00:52:37,144 --> 00:52:41,428
So when we begin Dharma practice, we are reminded not to forget sentient beings.

390
00:52:58,976 --> 00:53:08,024
So we kind of remind ourselves, I'm not going to do whatever practice or what have you that I'm about to embark on for myself alone.

391
00:53:08,745 --> 00:53:21,516
Rather, I'm going to do this with an altruistic attitude that encompasses all sentient beings and such for the sake of all sentient beings, I'm going to do whatever practice we're about to embark on.

392
00:53:23,102 --> 00:53:26,545
So with compassion we read, dedicate your merit to them.

393
00:53:26,565 --> 00:53:28,026
So here compassion.

394
00:53:28,186 --> 00:53:29,467
Compassion for whom?

395
00:53:29,487 --> 00:53:32,649
For all sentient beings, oneself included of course.

396
00:53:53,361 --> 00:54:06,845
Here, when we reflect upon oneself and all other sentient beings, we all are pretty much the same, in that we would like to have happiness and avoid discomfort or suffering.

397
00:54:08,265 --> 00:54:09,586
This is the lot for everybody.

398
00:54:10,246 --> 00:54:14,707
And if we notice, when we look around us, we'll find that each and every being

399
00:54:15,484 --> 00:54:24,930
Each and every sentient being is looking for happiness and that's why they are going here and there, doing this and that, trying to find some comfort with the hope that it will endure.

400
00:54:25,230 --> 00:54:28,292
Nobody rushes here or there with the hope of suffering now, do they?

401
00:54:44,243 --> 00:54:46,844
Happiness or peace or bliss, if you like.

402
00:54:46,864 --> 00:54:51,966
There's a variety of types, but the most sublime comes through the practice of the Dharma.

403
00:54:51,986 --> 00:54:53,427
We think about the Dharma then.

404
00:55:12,187 --> 00:55:16,569
hearing about it, contemplating it, and ultimately then putting it into practice.

405
00:55:17,050 --> 00:55:21,812
Putting it into practice then has the effect of subduing the unruly mind.

406
00:55:22,433 --> 00:55:30,797
Through the absolute subjugation of the unruly mind, one achieves then the level of the state of liberation.

407
00:55:31,097 --> 00:55:32,318
This then is the spiritual path.

408
00:55:41,028 --> 00:55:53,917
So when you think of others going out of their way to engage in practices that they hope will bring about their kind of peace and well-being, but are actually what we call erroneous paths.

409
00:55:54,858 --> 00:56:03,985
Paths in the sense that they're not going to bring about this ultimate happiness, but rather bring about that which they are trying to avoid, of the same, more dissatisfaction.

410
00:56:09,567 --> 00:56:11,589
So we are all interconnected in some ways.

411
00:56:11,669 --> 00:56:17,053
And so when we think about achieving liberation for oneself alone, one can't really break this connection.

412
00:56:44,539 --> 00:56:48,680
We can understand this when we think about our experiences of life.

413
00:56:49,521 --> 00:56:52,782
Any kind of happiness or comfort involves others.

414
00:56:54,202 --> 00:56:55,203
When we think about ourselves,

415
00:57:10,218 --> 00:57:12,439
When we were born, we didn't even have a name.

416
00:57:13,659 --> 00:57:16,220
Our name was given to us by somebody else.

417
00:57:17,100 --> 00:57:19,161
Then we were kind of clothed.

418
00:57:19,361 --> 00:57:22,182
Again, the clothes were given to us by somebody else.

419
00:57:22,622 --> 00:57:25,543
It's not as if we kind of had them packed up and we brought them with us.

420
00:57:27,564 --> 00:57:28,284
And we think about it.

421
00:57:35,422 --> 00:57:38,763
Where we might find ourselves, for example, here we are in a hall.

422
00:57:39,303 --> 00:57:40,684
The hall was built by other people.

423
00:57:40,704 --> 00:57:44,165
We didn't build it.

424
00:57:44,265 --> 00:58:00,290
As such, when we reflect that there is no way that we can have any experience completely devoid of involvement of another, then it helps us in opening our mind towards others.

425
00:58:05,883 --> 00:58:16,047
Accordingly, if we reflect upon past and future lives, in particular past lives, a life previous to this one, one previous to that, etc., then we know we can chase this back indefinitely.

426
00:58:32,955 --> 00:58:36,196
And during all those different lives, we had parents.

427
00:58:38,136 --> 00:58:49,780
Accordingly, when we equate the two, we could say, as mentioned, that there is not a single individual who has not at one time or another had the experience of being a kind parent to us.

428
00:59:07,062 --> 00:59:13,366
Accordingly, with compassion, dedicate your merit to those sentient beings and make prayers of aspiration.

429
00:59:14,107 --> 00:59:23,213
So when we engage in positive actions and we dedicate that merit, we should dedicate it then for the welfare of all.

430
00:59:32,190 --> 00:59:42,932
And so within these four lines, we find the quintessence of what we might call the Hinayana, Mahayana, and Vajrayana.

431
01:00:01,429 --> 01:00:04,650
In the past, when we were young, we used to go to the temple.

432
01:00:05,310 --> 01:00:06,990
When we were older, we used to go to the temple to pray.

433
01:00:07,090 --> 01:00:09,790
We used to go to the temple to pray.

434
01:00:09,811 --> 01:00:11,991
We used to go to the temple to pray.

435
01:00:12,031 --> 01:00:13,991
We used to go to the temple to pray.

436
01:00:14,011 --> 01:00:15,992
We used to go to the temple to pray.

437
01:00:16,012 --> 01:00:17,732
We used to go to the temple to pray.

438
01:00:31,003 --> 01:00:35,406
So if we look at the three lines once again.

439
01:00:36,968 --> 01:00:39,650
Do not forget death, let it spur you on to the Dharma.

440
01:00:39,850 --> 01:00:44,894
Through reflecting upon death and impermanence, we'll develop a sense of renunciation.

441
01:00:45,806 --> 01:00:51,130
Seeing the essencelessness of things will then spur us on to Dharma practice.

442
01:00:51,471 --> 01:00:56,935
Now this Dharma practice is not really something that one can do for oneself alone.

443
01:00:57,415 --> 01:01:01,719
As such, we read in the last line, do not forget sentient beings with compassion.

444
01:01:02,099 --> 01:01:05,021
Dedicate your merit to them and make prayers of aspiration.

445
01:01:05,642 --> 01:01:13,428
This includes then the quintessence of the Mahayana or Greater Vehicle Path to awaken for the benefit of all sentient beings.

446
01:01:14,033 --> 01:01:21,775
As such, one engages in practice with this extreme of altruism, the wish to awaken for the benefit of all sentient beings.

447
01:01:21,815 --> 01:01:25,496
And not being complacent, one then seeks out a spiritual teacher.

448
01:01:26,176 --> 01:01:27,737
Do not forget the guru, then we read.

449
01:01:27,937 --> 01:01:29,217
Pray to him at all times.

450
01:01:29,857 --> 01:01:35,979
And from this individual, we receive the quintessence of the Dharma, the Vajrayana, the kind of instructions.

451
01:01:37,099 --> 01:01:39,620
Having received them, putting them into practice, then we read,

452
01:01:40,309 --> 01:01:41,990
Do not let your mind be distracted.

453
01:01:42,450 --> 01:01:44,391
Look into its very essence.

454
01:01:44,451 --> 01:01:49,112
Hereby, we are able, upon this instruction, to engage the swift path.

455
01:01:49,633 --> 01:01:55,095
The swift path here, then, is the one which brings one's unruly mind quickly under control.

456
01:01:55,575 --> 01:02:00,617
As such, if one is without distraction, there is no longer an unruly mind.

457
01:02:00,717 --> 01:02:03,678
One has recognized its essence.

458
01:02:04,430 --> 01:02:08,613
And here, essentially, then, we are practicing the heart of the Dharma.

459
01:02:08,793 --> 01:02:14,716
Because, as the Buddha himself has said, all the teaching of the Buddha is to subdue the unruly mind.

460
01:02:15,036 --> 01:02:15,997
This is my teaching.

461
01:02:16,597 --> 01:02:30,446
Based thereupon, we find a variety of spiritual vehicles or methods, ways, if you will, of subduing the mind, based, respectively, on the practice of individual liberation, of the greater vehicle, and the Vādamāntaya Vajrayāna.

462
01:02:32,718 --> 01:02:36,982
In the past, when I was young, I used to go to school to learn how to read and write.

463
01:02:37,102 --> 01:02:41,506
When I grew up, I learned how to read and write.

464
01:02:41,526 --> 01:02:43,407
When I grew up, I learned how to read and write.

465
01:02:43,948 --> 01:02:46,150
When I grew up, I learned how to read and write.

466
01:02:46,170 --> 01:02:49,773
When I grew up, I learned how to read and write.

467
01:03:01,735 --> 01:03:20,523
So when we think of the way Buddhism was presented in the noble land of India, we find the approach of the three spiritual kind of vehicles, the lesser or the vows of the vehicle of individual liberation, the greater vehicle, the Advaita or Vajrayana vehicle.

468
01:03:21,947 --> 01:03:37,838
When this was imported into Tibet, we find different times and different translations, et cetera, which resulted in the four kind of traditions, Nyingma, Agyu, Sakya, Gelug, and so forth.

469
01:03:38,818 --> 01:03:47,224
When we look at all of these different externals, one should understand that they all have the same purpose, which is to subdue the unruly mind.

470
01:03:47,948 --> 01:03:55,711
When we think of the unruly mind, there are various ways to divide the different types of disturbing emotions that we all experience.

471
01:03:56,211 --> 01:04:02,973
Primarily, we can talk about hatred, desirous attachment or addiction, and confusion.

472
01:04:04,222 --> 01:04:08,044
And as such, then we have the various kind of practices to subdue them.

473
01:04:08,405 --> 01:04:16,089
And also then there is the extraordinary method to subdue them with one kind of swift blow, if you will.

474
01:04:16,630 --> 01:04:21,232
Accordingly, the Buddha then gave 84,000 different types of teaching.

475
01:04:21,312 --> 01:04:30,058
The reason for this was to address the unruly mind, such as addiction, aversion, pride, jealousy, confusion, and so forth.

476
01:04:32,947 --> 01:04:36,951
And so each spiritual tradition has its unique way of presenting the path.

477
01:04:36,991 --> 01:04:43,318
So for us, in Nyingma tradition, we speak of nine spiritual vehicles, or yanas.

478
01:04:56,424 --> 01:04:59,886
But if you don't have the ability to do it, you can't do it.

485
01:05:23,869 --> 01:05:29,831
So if we look at these nine jhanas, the first one is the vehicle of the shravakas, or hearers.

486
01:05:30,731 --> 01:05:39,234
And primarily, their method, or way, or type, if you will, of meditation is a reflection of the Four Noble Truths.

487
01:05:39,754 --> 01:05:51,477
So understanding the truth of the dukkha, understanding its origin, and the wish to abandon that through the implementation of a path which leads to its cessation.

488
01:05:52,018 --> 01:05:52,898
So here, then, we talk of,

489
01:05:53,568 --> 01:06:02,340
suffering, understanding the origin of suffering, wishing to bring about its cessation, and implying the path or method that will bring about that cessation, the Four-Level Truths.

490
01:06:03,101 --> 01:06:08,308
The second is the vehicle of the solitary realisers or Pratyekabuddhas,

491
01:06:08,928 --> 01:06:29,993
which in terms of their principle meditation is the reflection on the 12 links of mutual dependence going in a kind of terms of the depiction clockwise and in a reversed way in order to kind of end to see how one enters into cyclic existence and how one can emerge from that or reverse that if you will.

492
01:06:30,713 --> 01:06:34,134
This leads us then to the third vehicle which is of the Mahayana

493
01:06:34,807 --> 01:06:42,993
and within the Mahayana having given rise to the attitude of bodhichitta one engages then in the six perfect actions which result in the state of awakening.

494
01:06:43,874 --> 01:07:01,287
In this part of the Mahayana, it is said that Chiva, Chivata, Mambas, Chajju, Chujju, Lhasa, Gomiya

495
01:07:01,899 --> 01:07:05,901
And then we leave the Sutrayana and we go to the Vajrayana.

496
01:07:06,541 --> 01:07:07,301
So here the Vajrayana

497
01:07:18,953 --> 01:07:23,298
there are six practices or six spiritual vehicles.

498
01:07:23,799 --> 01:07:29,425
The first three, action, performance and yoga, are what we can generally call outer tantra.

499
01:07:29,565 --> 01:07:32,548
And then there are the three inner tantras.

500
01:07:33,169 --> 01:07:35,532
With regard to the first three then,

501
01:07:35,992 --> 01:07:43,874
There are the ways of meditation where one brings to mind the meditational deity in front of oneself and one makes offerings and so forth.

502
01:07:44,034 --> 01:07:52,476
And the example here is of oneself as a supplicant and the Buddha in front of one granting a spiritual kind of blessing or spiritual boons, if you will.

503
01:07:52,896 --> 01:07:58,498
Thereafter, one progresses through the practice becoming equal to the meditational deity.

504
01:07:58,978 --> 01:08:07,521
and this then, or the Buddha if you will, and this then leads to the inner tantra which is subdivided into three groups of Maha, Anu and Ati yoga respectively.

505
01:08:28,322 --> 01:08:34,487
So when we think of the practice of Maha Yoga, the principal meditation is creation phase yoga.

506
01:08:35,208 --> 01:08:39,731
With regard to Anu Yoga, the principal practice is a completion phase with characteristics.

507
01:08:40,252 --> 01:08:45,816
And for them, the last, Ati Yoga, and that is a completion phase without characteristics.

508
01:08:45,856 --> 01:08:54,563
So here, there is a gradation, a kind of a gradual path by which the one single aspirant practices so as to deepen their experience.

509
01:08:55,879 --> 01:09:06,627
And so we can continue to talk about each of these spiritual vehicles, but if we were to boil them down to their very essence, what we find here is the Heart Advice in four lines.

510
01:09:21,610 --> 01:09:29,938
this four-line aspiration, prayer or supplication, if you will, contains the quintessence of the entire Buddhadharma.

511
01:09:46,505 --> 01:09:55,129
For example, when we think about a creation phase practice or sadhana practice, if you like, we have the Lama, we have trust in the Lama.

512
01:09:55,229 --> 01:10:00,331
The Lama gives us empowerment and we thereby are introduced to the Yidam deity.

513
01:10:00,511 --> 01:10:05,313
When we meditate upon the Yidam deity, the Yidam deity isn't separate from the Lama.

514
01:10:05,414 --> 01:10:06,594
The two are indivisible.

515
01:10:07,174 --> 01:10:13,877
So by having trust in the Lama, one is meditating upon the Lama as indivisible from the meditation deity.

516
01:10:14,438 --> 01:10:15,278
And that is the path.

517
01:10:16,167 --> 01:10:21,209
And the very painful spiritual path is Atiyoga, the Great Perfection, Zopa Shambho.

518
01:10:21,229 --> 01:10:24,791
For this, the Lama introduces the awakened nature to the student.

519
01:10:42,405 --> 01:10:51,030
through receiving that introduction, then one begins to kind of digest it, to recognize that which has been introduced.

520
01:10:51,050 --> 01:10:57,134
And this is then the very pinnacle of the practice, which is kind of essentialized in the second line.

521
01:11:15,419 --> 01:11:23,044
So when we think about the practice of Atiyoga, it seems very simple, doesn't it?

522
01:11:23,625 --> 01:11:28,809
The nature is introduced, and then you simply recognize that, and you are Buddha.

523
01:11:29,669 --> 01:11:37,335
But here, in order for that recognition to occur, there has to have been, on the side of the supplement or the practitioner,

524
01:11:37,933 --> 01:11:48,858
an inconceivable amount of purification of previously engaged negative actions, and the accumulation of an inconceivable amount of positive potential or merit.

525
01:11:49,118 --> 01:11:53,820
For without this, no matter how many introductions are made, a recognition will not occur.

526
01:12:06,720 --> 01:12:14,922
And so the point of all of these spiritual practices is to bring the student to a certain level of spiritual maturity

527
01:12:35,484 --> 01:12:38,046
to make them ready for this introduction.

528
01:12:38,866 --> 01:12:42,429
And the introduction itself doesn't have to be particularly elaborate.

529
01:12:43,189 --> 01:12:48,413
Neither does it have to be from a text or involving a ritual at all.

530
01:12:49,033 --> 01:12:56,979
I'm sure you're familiar with the story of Bardor Rinpoche introducing the nature of mind to his disciple, Wushu Lungdrup.

531
01:12:57,279 --> 01:13:02,683
At the time, they were lying on their backs, and Bardor Rinpoche said,

532
01:13:03,618 --> 01:13:05,179
Do you see the stars in the sky?"

533
01:13:05,860 --> 01:13:06,880
Lungdok replied, yes.

534
01:13:07,000 --> 01:13:09,002
He said, do you hear the dogs barking below?

535
01:13:09,522 --> 01:13:10,102
He said, yes.

536
01:13:10,122 --> 01:13:12,104
He said, that's his mind.

537
01:13:12,724 --> 01:13:19,908
And at that point, because of his prior training, he recognized the nature of mind that had been introduced, that was introduced.

539
01:13:32,167 --> 01:13:36,768
So for those of you who take the tube, then you'll know the expression, mind the gap, right?

540
01:13:38,968 --> 01:13:47,790
I don't know how to explain better.

541
01:13:51,570 --> 01:13:52,451
So I think that's it.

542
01:13:54,331 --> 01:13:54,511
What?

543
01:13:54,571 --> 01:13:56,251
Does it do that?

544
01:13:56,611 --> 01:13:56,951
Okay.

545
01:13:58,772 --> 01:14:00,272
Okay, so little kind of

546
01:14:02,384 --> 01:14:30,433
comedy joke finish so thank you very much through this note may all beings attain the omniscient state of enlightenment and conquer the enemy of false and delusion may they all be liberated from this ocean of samsara and from its pounding waves of birth old age sickness and

547
01:14:31,635 --> 01:14:42,358
Just as the Bodhisattva Manjushri attained omniscience and Samantabhadra too, all these merits now I dedicate to train and follow in their footsteps.

548
01:14:43,418 --> 01:14:57,902
As all the victorious Buddhas of past, present and future praise dedication as supreme, so now I too dedicate all these sources of merit of mine for all beings to perfect good actions.

549
01:14:59,779 --> 01:15:05,262
Prayer for the long life of Digno Khyentse II of Session, Urgyen Tenzing Jigme Lundrup.

550
01:15:06,683 --> 01:15:07,563
Om Svati.

551
01:15:08,263 --> 01:15:24,432
Through the compassion of the infinite Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, and the blessing of wondrous Gurus, Devas and Dakinis, the beloved Master Khyentse, treasure of knowledge and love, has manifested his matchless compassion, just as all have wished.

552
01:15:25,452 --> 01:15:28,234
Fearless holder of the teachings of the Lord of Urgyen,

553
01:15:28,881 --> 01:15:45,706
and of the non-partisan tradition of sutras and tantras, all-victorious one, let your life be forever firm and indestructible, and accomplish spontaneously and without effort your vision of present happiness and ultimate bliss.

554
01:15:47,487 --> 01:15:50,308
Prayer for the long life of Shetanarath Jamyonposhche.

555
01:15:51,448 --> 01:15:56,650
The tradition of Samantabhadra and Padmakara is the great perfection in Sogpachempo.

556
01:15:57,492 --> 01:16:03,175
For its teachings you are a brilliant sun beyond compare, infinite in your wisdom.

557
01:16:04,056 --> 01:16:13,801
Peerless great lion of the Dharma, of teaching and practice, may your secret body, speech and mind remain steadfast and indestructible.

558
01:16:15,442 --> 01:16:20,525
A great drum to invoke compassion, a prayer for Sogyal Rinpoche's swift rebirth.

559
01:16:21,913 --> 01:16:34,483
Vajradhara, sixth lord endowed with eight special powers, Avalokiteshvara and Tara sublime, lotus-born Buddha, great treasure revealers and your heirs, to you all we pray.

560
01:16:35,043 --> 01:16:36,844
Think of us this very instant.

561
01:16:37,805 --> 01:16:49,174
The merit you gathered so completely is like a brilliant lotus flower, its heart adorned with a lineage that matures and liberates and resplendent on a vast ocean of instructions.

562
01:16:50,010 --> 01:16:54,173
You that raised the victory banner of Buddhist teachings, to you we pray.

563
01:16:55,254 --> 01:17:02,700
Though you have the power to teach Dharma in all kinds of ways, you saw no way to bring further benefit at a time such as this.

564
01:17:03,560 --> 01:17:11,947
You chose instead to seek the tranquility of Samadhi and let your vibrant wisdom mind enter the still forest of the Dharmadhatu.

565
01:17:12,860 --> 01:17:30,065
But for all those who are drawn to what is profound and vast, we pray that you return swiftly, a youthful sun, a supreme emanation, with a thousand rays of light bringing teachings for this time, the divine and sacred 84,000 teachings of Dharma.

566
01:17:31,105 --> 01:17:39,828
And when you return in a form to match your predecessor, Tertön, a Lord of Siddhas, adorned with all the beauty of a perfect life of liberation,

567
01:17:40,627 --> 01:17:48,169
May you ripen into a splendor to behold, a vision for the eyes of thousands of receptive and fortunate disciples.

568
01:17:50,970 --> 01:17:52,670
The prayer in six Vajra lines.

569
01:17:54,091 --> 01:18:05,814
Embodiment of Buddhas of past, present and future, Guru Rinpoche, master of all siddhis, guru of great bliss, dispeller of all obstacles, wrathful subjugator of Maras,

570
01:18:06,477 --> 01:18:17,589
To you I pray, inspire me with your blessing, so that outer, inner and secret obstacles are dispelled, and all my aspirations are spontaneously fulfilled.

571
01:18:18,390 --> 01:18:22,154
Aum Ma Hum Ben Sa Guru Pema Siddhi Hum.

572
01:18:23,995 --> 01:18:26,338
Prayer for the tradition of Guru Padmasambhava.

573
01:18:27,337 --> 01:18:37,865
May the living tradition of Guru Padmasambhava, Bodhisattva Shantarakshita, and the Dharma King, Sri Sanderson, spread throughout the world in all directions.

574
01:18:38,646 --> 01:18:48,253
May the Buddha, Dharma, and Sangha be present in the minds of all, inseparably at all times, bringing peace, happiness, and well-being.

575
01:19:02,782 --> 01:19:10,567
Ramesh, I firstly would like to extend my thanks to all of the volunteers who have helped make this evening possible.

576
01:19:10,607 --> 01:19:28,598
So I just wanted to thank all of the stewards, to the folks who work in the shop, reception, the wonderful team behind the audiovisual desk, the attendants, the organizing team, and the administration and registration team, without which we wouldn't be able to do this.

577
01:19:28,658 --> 01:19:30,520
So thank you to all of the volunteers.

578
01:19:30,580 --> 01:19:30,980
Thank you.

579
01:19:33,232 --> 01:19:44,238
Rinpoche, you spoke with warmth of your master, Dilga Khyentse Rinpoche, your kind, loving grandfather who became your spiritual teacher.

580
01:19:45,599 --> 01:19:53,843
You talked with clarity, urging us to digest the instructions in order for our mind not to be distracted.

581
01:19:54,780 --> 01:20:06,509
that we might recognize change and death and have a desire to emerge swiftly from samsara while not forgetting the kindness of sentient beings.

582
01:20:08,451 --> 01:20:20,100
In order to do that, your teaching this evening has reminded us that we must rely on an authentic spiritual master, a source of inspiration, the representation of the Dharma.

583
01:20:22,345 --> 01:20:28,530
To that end, we pray that, unlike the guns you spoke of, may your teachings always hit the target.

584
01:20:31,151 --> 01:20:39,337
And of all the top football players in England, dare I say you embody not only the goalkeeper, the defence, the midfield and the striker.

585
01:20:40,498 --> 01:20:41,239
Thank God.

586
01:20:49,578 --> 01:20:52,899
Rinpoche, we pray for your happiness and good health.

587
01:20:53,839 --> 01:20:58,321
May all of your aspirations and good works come to full fruition.

588
01:20:59,261 --> 01:21:03,462
Yours is a kind and direct voice which is very, very necessary.

589
01:21:04,403 --> 01:21:06,924
We urge you to remain a close friend of Rigpa.

590
01:21:07,684 --> 01:21:11,825
Return to London soon and bless us again with your presence and your teachings.

591
01:21:12,825 --> 01:21:17,567
We would also like to thank the Venerable Sean for your eloquent and warm translation.

592
01:21:18,595 --> 01:21:24,824
On behalf of all of us here, we offer you our thanks and gratitude for this wonderful evening of teaching.

593
01:21:24,864 --> 01:21:25,225
Thank you.

